  "atlasUlnaSummary": "The medial forearm bone, on the little-finger side. Forms the hinge of the elbow and is the stable partner the radius rotates around.",
  "@atlasUlnaSummary": {
    "description": "Atlas, anatomy text for experts. Keep the Latin terms where your language uses them"
  },
  "atlasUlnaLm1": "Olecranon process — the point of the elbow",
  "@atlasUlnaLm1": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasUlnaLm2": "Trochlear notch gripping the humerus",
  "@atlasUlnaLm2": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasUlnaLm3": "Coronoid process below the notch",
  "@atlasUlnaLm3": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasUlnaLm4": "Slender distal head with styloid process",
  "@atlasUlnaLm4": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasUlnaSiding": "The trochlear notch opens anteriorly; with the olecranon up and the notch facing you, the shaft curves toward the body side.",
  "@atlasUlnaSiding": {
    "description": "Atlas: how to tell left from right, or the place in a series"
  },
  "atlasScapulaBlurb": "Shoulder blade; thin triangular plate",
  "@atlasScapulaBlurb": {
    "description": "Atlas: one line under the bone name in the list"
  },
  "atlasScapulaSummary": "The shoulder blade — a thin triangular plate riding on the back of the ribcage. Thin enough that the blade often survives only as fragments.",
  "@atlasScapulaSummary": {
    "description": "Atlas, anatomy text for experts. Keep the Latin terms where your language uses them"
  },
  "atlasScapulaLm1": "Spine running across the posterior surface",
  "@atlasScapulaLm1": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasScapulaLm2": "Acromion process at the shoulder tip",
  "@atlasScapulaLm2": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasScapulaLm3": "Hooked coracoid process anteriorly",
  "@atlasScapulaLm3": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasScapulaLm4": "Glenoid fossa receiving the humeral head",
  "@atlasScapulaLm4": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasScapulaSiding": "The glenoid fossa faces laterally and the spine sits on the posterior surface; the side the fossa faces is the side of the body.",
  "@atlasScapulaSiding": {
    "description": "Atlas: how to tell left from right, or the place in a series"
  },
  "atlasOsCoxaBlurb": "Hip bone; best indicator of sex",
  "@atlasOsCoxaBlurb": {
    "description": "Atlas: one line under the bone name in the list"
  },
  "atlasOsCoxaSummary": "The hip bone: ilium, ischium and pubis fused at the acetabulum. The single most reliable element for estimating sex and adult age.",
  "@atlasOsCoxaSummary": {
    "description": "Atlas, anatomy text for experts. Keep the Latin terms where your language uses them"
  },
  "atlasOsCoxaLm1": "Greater sciatic notch — broad in females, narrow in males",
  "@atlasOsCoxaLm1": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasOsCoxaLm2": "Subpubic angle and the ventral arc",
  "@atlasOsCoxaLm2": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasOsCoxaLm3": "Auricular surface, used for age-at-death",
  "@atlasOsCoxaLm3": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasOsCoxaLm4": "Acetabulum receiving the femoral head",
  "@atlasOsCoxaLm4": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasOsCoxaSiding": "Point the iliac blade up and the acetabulum out: the pubis sits anteriorly and the sciatic notch opens posteriorly.",
  "@atlasOsCoxaSiding": {
    "description": "Atlas: how to tell left from right, or the place in a series"
  },
  "atlasOsCoxaAka": "Innominate, hip bone",
  "@atlasOsCoxaAka": {
    "description": "Atlas: another name for this bone"
  },
  "atlasRib12Blurb": "Atypical ribs; flat and sharply curved",
  "@atlasRib12Blurb": {
    "description": "Atlas: one line under the bone name in the list"
  },
  "atlasRib12Summary": "The first two ribs are atypical. Rib 1 is the shortest, broadest and most sharply curved; rib 2 is longer but keeps the same flattened set.",
  "@atlasRib12Summary": {
    "description": "Atlas, anatomy text for experts. Keep the Latin terms where your language uses them"
  },
  "atlasRib12Lm1": "Rib 1 lies flat, with superior and inferior surfaces",
  "@atlasRib12Lm1": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasRib12Lm2": "Scalene tubercle with grooves for the subclavian vessels",
  "@atlasRib12Lm2": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasRib12Lm3": "Rib 2 carries a tuberosity for serratus anterior",
  "@atlasRib12Lm3": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasRib12Lm4": "Single articular facet on the head of rib 1",
  "@atlasRib12Lm4": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasRib12Siding": "Lay the rib flat with the head backward: the concave inner surface faces the midline, giving the side.",
  "@atlasRib12Siding": {
    "description": "Atlas: how to tell left from right, or the place in a series"
  },
  "atlasTypicalRibBlurb": "Ribs 3–9; head, tubercle, costal groove",
  "@atlasTypicalRibBlurb": {
    "description": "Atlas: one line under the bone name in the list"
  },
  "atlasTypicalRibSummary": "Ribs 3 through 9 share one plan: a head, neck, tubercle and a long curving body that twists at the angle.",
  "@atlasTypicalRibSummary": {
    "description": "Atlas, anatomy text for experts. Keep the Latin terms where your language uses them"
  },
  "atlasTypicalRibLm1": "Two demifacets on the head",
  "@atlasTypicalRibLm1": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasTypicalRibLm2": "Tubercle articulating with a transverse process",
  "@atlasTypicalRibLm2": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasTypicalRibLm3": "Angle where the shaft changes direction",
  "@atlasTypicalRibLm3": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasTypicalRibLm4": "Costal groove along the inner inferior border",
  "@atlasTypicalRibLm4": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasTypicalRibSiding": "The costal groove runs on the internal inferior edge; with the groove down and inside, the head points to the back and gives the side.",
  "@atlasTypicalRibSiding": {
    "description": "Atlas: how to tell left from right, or the place in a series"
  },
  "atlasRib1112Blurb": "Floating ribs; short and unattached",
  "@atlasRib1112Blurb": {
    "description": "Atlas: one line under the bone name in the list"
  },
  "atlasRib1112Summary": "The floating ribs. Short, tapering and unattached at the front, which is why they lack most of the features a typical rib carries.",
  "@atlasRib1112Summary": {
    "description": "Atlas, anatomy text for experts. Keep the Latin terms where your language uses them"
  },
  "atlasRib1112Lm1": "Single articular facet on the head",
  "@atlasRib1112Lm1": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasRib1112Lm2": "No neck and no tubercle",
  "@atlasRib1112Lm2": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasRib1112Lm3": "Little or no costal groove",
  "@atlasRib1112Lm3": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasRib1112Lm4": "Pointed, cartilage-tipped anterior end",
  "@atlasRib1112Lm4": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasRib1112Siding": "Curvature alone gives the side: the concave surface faces the midline.",
  "@atlasRib1112Siding": {
    "description": "Atlas: how to tell left from right, or the place in a series"
  },
  "atlasCervicalBlurb": "C1–C7; the only ones with transverse foramina",
  "@atlasCervicalBlurb": {
    "description": "Atlas: one line under the bone name in the list"
  },
  "atlasCervicalSummary": "The seven neck vertebrae, C1–C7. Smallest bodies in the column and the only vertebrae with holes in the transverse processes.",
  "@atlasCervicalSummary": {
    "description": "Atlas, anatomy text for experts. Keep the Latin terms where your language uses them"
  },
  "atlasCervicalLm1": "Transverse foramina — diagnostic for the region",
  "@atlasCervicalLm1": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasCervicalLm2": "C1 (atlas) has no body; C2 (axis) carries the dens",
  "@atlasCervicalLm2": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasCervicalLm3": "Bifid spinous processes on C2–C6",
  "@atlasCervicalLm3": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasCervicalLm4": "Large triangular vertebral foramen",
  "@atlasCervicalLm4": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasCervicalSiding": "Symmetrical. Position in the series is read from the spinous process and body size; C7 has the long, unsplit \"prominens\" spine.",
  "@atlasCervicalSiding": {
    "description": "Atlas: how to tell left from right, or the place in a series"
  },
  "atlasThoracicBlurb": "T1–T12; carry the costal facets",
  "@atlasThoracicBlurb": {
    "description": "Atlas: one line under the bone name in the list"
  },
  "atlasThoracicSummary": "The twelve vertebrae of the upper back, T1–T12. Each one articulates with a pair of ribs, and the facets for those ribs identify the region.",
  "@atlasThoracicSummary": {
    "description": "Atlas, anatomy text for experts. Keep the Latin terms where your language uses them"
  },
  "atlasThoracicLm1": "Costal facets on the body and transverse processes",
  "@atlasThoracicLm1": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasThoracicLm2": "Heart-shaped vertebral bodies",
  "@atlasThoracicLm2": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasThoracicLm3": "Long spinous processes angled steeply downward",
  "@atlasThoracicLm3": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasThoracicLm4": "Circular vertebral foramen",
  "@atlasThoracicLm4": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasThoracicSiding": "Symmetrical. Sequence is judged from body size and how far the spinous process overlaps the vertebra below.",
  "@atlasThoracicSiding": {
    "description": "Atlas: how to tell left from right, or the place in a series"
  },
  "atlasLumbarBlurb": "L1–L5; the heaviest in the column",
  "@atlasLumbarBlurb": {
    "description": "Atlas: one line under the bone name in the list"
  },
  "atlasLumbarSummary": "The five vertebrae of the lower back, L1–L5. The largest and most robust in the column, built for load rather than movement.",
  "@atlasLumbarSummary": {
    "description": "Atlas, anatomy text for experts. Keep the Latin terms where your language uses them"
  },
  "atlasLumbarLm1": "Massive kidney-shaped bodies",
  "@atlasLumbarLm1": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasLumbarLm2": "No transverse foramina and no costal facets",
  "@atlasLumbarLm2": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasLumbarLm3": "Short, blunt, hatchet-shaped spinous processes",
  "@atlasLumbarLm3": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasLumbarLm4": "Mammillary processes on the superior articular facets",
  "@atlasLumbarLm4": {
    "description": "Atlas: one thing an expert looks for on this bone"
  },
  "atlasLumbarSiding": "Symmetrical. L5 is recognised by its wedge shape, deeper in front than behind, where it meets the sacrum.",
  "@atlasLumbarSiding": {
    "description": "Atlas: how to tell left from right, or the place in a series"
  },
  "phaseDoc1Title": "Is there a bone?",
  "@phaseDoc1Title": {
    "description": "How analysis works: step title"
  },
  "phaseDoc1Detail": "First Ossia checks there's a bone in the photo at all, so a stick or a stone doesn't get a made-up answer.",
  "@phaseDoc1Detail": {
    "description": "How analysis works: what this step does"
  },
  "phaseDoc2Title": "Human or animal?",
  "@phaseDoc2Title": {
    "description": "How analysis works: step title"
  },
  "phaseDoc2Detail": "Next it decides whether the bone came from a person or an animal. Everything after depends on this step.",
  "@phaseDoc2Detail": {
    "description": "How analysis works: what this step does"
  },
  "phaseDoc3Title": "Which bone?",
  "@phaseDoc3Title": {
    "description": "How analysis works: step title"
  },
  "phaseDoc3Detail": "Sixteen kinds of bone, from the skull and jaw to the ribs, spine, hip and limbs. You see its top three guesses, not just the winner.",
  "@phaseDoc3Detail": {
    "description": "How analysis works: what this step does"
  },
  "phaseDoc4Title": "Which part?",
  "@phaseDoc4Title": {
    "description": "How analysis works: step title"
  },
  "phaseDoc4Detail": "Whether the photo shows the whole bone, its middle (the shaft), one end, or a broken piece.",
  "@phaseDoc4Detail": {
    "description": "How analysis works: what this step does"
  },
  "phaseDoc5Title": "Whole or broken?",
  "@phaseDoc5Title": {
    "description": "How analysis works: step title"
  },
  "phaseDoc5Detail": "A quick yes or no: is the bone complete, or only a fragment?",
  "@phaseDoc5Detail": {
    "description": "How analysis works: what this step does"
  },
  "phaseDoc6Title": "Marks and damage",
  "@phaseDoc6Title": {
    "description": "How analysis works: step title"
  },
  "phaseDoc6Detail": "Breaks, cut marks and burning are each checked on their own, so one bone can show all three.",
  "@phaseDoc6Detail": {
    "description": "How analysis works: what this step does"
  },
  "phaseDoc7Title": "Which animal?",
  "@phaseDoc7Title": {
    "description": "How analysis works: step title"
  },
  "phaseDoc7Detail": "For animal bones: mammal, bird or reptile. For mammals, also the family, like the dog, deer, cattle, pig, cat, bear, rabbit, raccoon or horse family, or a rodent. The family is only named when the bone can show it, such as a skull, a jaw or a whole leg bone.",
  "@phaseDoc7Detail": {
    "description": "How analysis works: what this step does"
  },
  "phaseDoc8Title": "Age and sex",
  "@phaseDoc8Title": {
    "description": "How analysis works: step title"
  },
  "phaseDoc8Detail": "For human bones only. Treat these as a hint to check, never as an answer on their own.",
  "@phaseDoc8Detail": {
    "description": "How analysis works: what this step does"
  },
  "phaseDoc9Title": "Putting it together",
  "@phaseDoc9Title": {
    "description": "How analysis works: step title"
  },
  "phaseDoc9Detail": "Photos of the same bone from different sides are read together, and the length you measured is checked against the bone's usual size. Both help when one photo alone is unclear.",
  "@phaseDoc9Detail": {
    "description": "How analysis works: what this step does"
  },
